# Structured Agents Project Conventions

## Repository Structure

- `agents/<name>/` -- agent definitions with `agent.yaml` (metadata) + `AGENT.md` (instructions)
- `skills/<name>/` -- reusable capabilities with `SKILL.md` (frontmatter + instructions)
- `profiles/<name>.yaml` -- known repository profiles
- `mcps/<name>.yaml` -- MCP server configurations
- `remote/` -- SSH remote execution config
- `templates/` -- templates for creating new agents, skills, profiles
- `src/structured_agents/` -- Python runtime (CLI + MCP server)

## Creating New Agents

1. Create directory under `agents/<agent-name>/`
2. Add `agent.yaml` following `agents/_schema.yaml`
3. Add `AGENT.md` with system prompt and instructions
4. Register in parent agent's `sub_agents` list if it should be delegated to

## Creating New Skills

1. Create directory under `skills/<skill-name>/`
2. Add `SKILL.md` with YAML frontmatter (`name`, `description`, `tools`, `mcps`)
3. Keep under 150 lines; use progressive disclosure for complex skills
4. Register in agent `agent.yaml` `skills` lists where appropriate

## Key Patterns

- All remote execution goes through SSH to the remote server (see `remote/server.yaml`)
- Agent names use kebab-case matching their directory name
- Skills are referenced by name (directory name) in agent.yaml files
- Repo profiles provide built-in knowledge; `repo-discover` skill supplements at runtime
- Context flows through filesystem workspace + structured YAML/JSON artifacts

## Python Code Style

- Python 3.12+, Ruff for linting, mypy strict mode
- Click for CLI, Pydantic for data models, PyYAML for config loading
- Hatchling for packaging
